  11. Patents.

  A "contributor" is a copyright holder who authorizes use under this
License of the Program or a work on which the Program is based.  The
work thus licensed is called the contributor's "contributor version".

  A contributor's "essential patent claims" are all patent claims
owned or controlled by the contributor, whether already acquired or
hereafter acquired, that would be infringed by some manner, permitted
by this License, of making, using, or selling its contributor version,
but do not include claims that would be infringed only as a
consequence of further modification of the contributor version.  In
this license, "patent claim" means any claim in any patent or patent
application, as well as any claim that might be renewed or extended.

  An "initiator" is a contributor who, with respect to a patent license
under this section, publishes in some manner the availability of that
patent license.  For purposes of this section, "you" are the initiator
if you publish a patent license under this section.

  For each of your essential patent claims arising from your
contributors, the following conditions apply:

    a) If you initiate a patent license, you must grant a worldwide,
    royalty-free license under the essential patent claims (subject to
    the provisions of section 11) that is not subject to any
    restriction on modification or further licensing, and that is
    available to anyone who comes into possession of a copy of the
    work, unless you have made a determination that the patent claim
    has been infringed by a particular act of modification or further
    licensing of the work.

    b) If you do not initiate a patent license, your essential patent
    claims are licensed as provided in section 6, on the condition
    that your essential patent claims are subject to section 3.

  An initiator's patent license under this section is defensive; the
initiator may, in whole or in part, invoke this patent license only
with respect to claims of patent infringement that the initiator
reasonably believes to be part of the set of essential patent claims
for which the initiator has initiated a patent license.  An initiator
who merely includes a patented invention in a contributor version does
not thereby initiate a patent license under this section; the
initiator must take some additional positive act.

  If you are a contributor, and you initiate a patent license, then you
grant a worldwide, royalty-free license in accordance with the
following:

  (1) Basic License.  Any licensee, in good faith, is hereby granted
  a royalty-free, worldwide license under your essential patent claims,
  subject to the provisions of this section 11, to make, use, sell,
  offer for sale, import, and otherwise run, modify, and propagate the
  contributor version, as well as works based on it.

  (2) Reciprocal License.  As a condition of the grant of the Basic
  License, each licensee of your essential patent claims, for each work
  that they licensee, must grant to you and all other licensees, a
  royalty-free, worldwide license under any essential patent claims they
  own or control, that are applicable to the contributor version or
  works based on it.

  (3) Defensive License.  The license granted under this section 11 is a
  defensive license.  An initiator of a patent license may invoke the
  license only against a party who files a patent infringement lawsuit
  against the initiator, or who has directly promised, threatened, or
  contended that the initiator's conduct infringes the initiator's
  essential patent claims.

